"I am writing to apply for the Data Scientist position at Octopus Energy Trading. I am currently completing an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London, after graduating from Imperial College London. Across my academic and professional experience, I have built forecasting and machine learning models in Python, worked with large and often messy datasets, and built the pipelines that feed those models rather than relying on someone else to supply them. I am particularly interested in this role because it combines forecasting, machine learning and production data work with a purpose I care about.",

"What attracts me most to Octopus Energy is the opportunity to build and maintain models that forecast electricity demand and renewable generation for the GB intraday power market, and to bring together internal and external data to do it. I enjoy taking an unclear business problem, identifying what users actually need, and turning it into a workflow that can be tested and improved. I do not come from the electricity industry, which the posting notes is useful but not required, and I would need to learn the market fundamentals properly. What I can offer is a habit of building a simple baseline first, iterating quickly, and being clear about what a forecast can and cannot support. My interest in the transition to Net Zero comes from a biology background and from volunteering as an Urban Nature Volunteer at the Natural History Museum.",

"In my current role at TikTok Shop UK, I query, clean and explore large merchant and transaction datasets in SQL and Python, and I build and maintain the pipelines and feature logic behind recurring analysis. I build AI agents that automate recurring analytical and reporting workflows, using Claude Code as part of my day-to-day development. I also define the KPI frameworks and dashboards that operations teams use to set targets, and present findings to product and commercial stakeholders, including where the numbers are uncertain.",

"In my previous role at DS Digital Technology, I built the Python pipeline behind a patient-facing medical agent for a pharmaceutical client, connecting six model APIs with retrieval augmentation and implementing concurrency, retries, structured validation and checkpointing so that long automated runs completed reliably and could resume after failure. Building in checks and alerting was the difference between a process that ran unattended and one that quietly produced bad output. I also co-built the knowledge base behind the agent, a medically reviewed knowledge graph of 123 entities and 161 relations, and designed blinded paired A/B tests to measure whether grounding actually improved answer quality rather than assuming that it would.",

"My MSc dissertation gave me experience with predictive modelling across the full lifecycle. Using longitudinal biobank data, I engineered features across multiple time points and compared logistic regression against three tree ensembles to predict cardiovascular events in 458,840 participants. Discrimination was modest, with AUC between 0.714 and 0.718, but calibration was close to exact, which is what makes a predicted probability usable for a decision. I then estimated individual outcomes under different interventions using doubly robust causal forests, tested the sensitivity of those results to unmeasured confounding, and deployed an interactive Streamlit tool that lets a user vary the inputs and see the modelled effect. Several results were null, and I reported them as null.",

"My earlier experience as a Strategy Analyst Intern at Ipsos Strategy3 gave me direct forecasting experience in a commercial setting. I built market-sizing and forecasting models identifying approximately \\$20M in revenue potential, structured evidence from more than 80 expert interviews into comparable variables, and worked with research and strategy colleagues to turn analytical findings into executive dashboards and client recommendations. This taught me to pick up unfamiliar commercial and industry concepts quickly and to explain a forecast to people who will act on it.",

"I would bring Octopus Energy strong Python, practical machine learning and forecasting experience, a willingness to own the data pipeline end to end, and genuine enthusiasm for contributing to production code. I hold the right to work in the UK without sponsorship until January 2029 and I am based in London.",

"Thank you for considering my application. I would welcome the opportunity to discuss how my experience aligns with the Data Scientist role and the work of the Trading team.",
